.TH CSVFILL 1
.SH NAME
csvfill \- fill missing values in a CSV table from another
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B csvfill
.RB [ \-d
.IR delim ]
.RB [ \-1
.IR col1 ]
.RB [ \-2
.IR col2 ]
.I file1
.RI [ file2 ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B csvfill
reads two delimited tables,
.I file1
and
.IR file2 ,
each beginning with a header row.
Rows are paired by an ID column (the first column by default; see
.B \-1
and
.BR \-2 ).
Columns are matched by name, not position.
Fields of
.I file1
that are missing \(em that is, empty or exactly
.B NA
or
.B NaN
\(em are replaced with the value from the matching row of
.IR file2 ;
the ID column of
.I file2
is not itself copied.
The result is written to standard output.
If
.I file2
is omitted, it is read from standard input.
.PP
It is an error if
.IP \(bu 2
an ID appears more than once in either table;
.IP \(bu
an ID in
.I file2
has no matching row in
.IR file1 ;
.IP \(bu
a column of
.I file2
does not exist in
.IR file1 ;
.IP \(bu
a row has a different number of fields than its header, or contains
unbalanced double quotes; or
.IP \(bu
a field that is not missing would be overwritten.
.PP
Fields may be enclosed in double quotes to protect an embedded delimiter.
Lines containing no delimiter are ignored.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-d " delim"
Use
.I delim
as the field delimiter instead of a comma.
It must be a single character other than the double quote.
.TP
.BI \-1 " col1"
Number (counting from 1) of the ID column in
.IR file1 .
The default is 1.
.TP
.BI \-2 " col2"
Number of the ID column in
.IR file2 .
The default is 1.
.TP
.B \-h
Print a usage summary and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Exits 0 on success, 1 on error.
.SH EXAMPLES
Fill missing entries of
.I people.csv
from
.IR updates.csv :
.PP
.RS
.nf
 $ csvfill people.csv updates.csv > people.complete.csv
.fi
.RE
.PP
Semicolon-delimited, ID in column 2 of the master, second table on stdin:
.PP
.RS
.nf
 $ csvfill -d ';' -1 2 master.csv < patch.csv
.fi
.RE
